Feed X11 XInput 2 touch into GPUI's TouchEvent and drop the pointer events the server emulates from it.

What: gpui_linux's X11 client, ported from winit v0.30.13 (e9809ef54b18499bb4f2cac945719ecc2a61061b)
`src/platform_impl/linux/x11/event_processor.rs` (`XI_Touch*` arm, `xinput2_touch`, the `XIPointerEmulated` check
of `xinput2_button_input`) and `window.rs` (the touch masks), Apache-2.0:
- Windows select XI_TouchBegin / Update / End on the master devices, with the pointer and gesture masks, from one
  table by XInput version (`XinputVersion::window_events`: touch from 2.2, pinch gestures from 2.4). The client's
  `supports_xinput_gestures: bool` becomes the negotiated `XinputVersion`, so a server older than 2.2 never gets a
  touch mask (it would fail the whole selection).
- XinputTouchBegin / Update / End → `PlatformInput::Touch` (`touch_from_xinput`, table `XI2_TOUCH_PHASES`): the
  detail is the `TouchId` (XI2 touch ids are globally unique and increase per touch), `device: Touch`, the position
  in logical pixels as the pointer events convert theirs, `time_stamp` from the server's ms `time` moved onto GPUI's
  clock by its age (`X11Clock`: Xorg and Xwayland stamp with CLOCK_MONOTONIC ms, read locally the same way; an age
  over 10 s either way means a remote server's clock: `None`, timed at dispatch). No `Cancelled`, as in winit and
  GDK: without XI_TouchOwnership a client gets a touch only once it owns it, and an owned touch ends only with
  XI_TouchEnd.
- XinputButtonPress / Release / Motion with `POINTER_EMULATED` are dropped, as winit drops them: those the server
  makes from a touch, so a tap clicks once (GPUI makes its own mouse events from the touch), and the scroll-button
  clicks emulated from smooth scrolling, which upstream already skipped (its check inside the scroll arm goes). The
  dropped emulated scroll clicks no longer reset the IME composition, as real clicks do.
- Table tests in x11/event.rs: XI2 touch event fixtures → TouchEvent, X11 time stamps (wrap, foreign clock), masks
  per XInput version, the emulation filter.

Pen: X11 tablets send pointer events with pressure valuators, not touches, and GPUI's mouse events carry no
device (docs/pointer-source.md), so a pen stays `Mouse`, as on the web. Deferred.

Why: upstream's X11 backend selects no touch events, so a touchscreen only drives the server's emulated pointer: no
TouchEvent, no gestures, no multi-touch. Selecting touch and dropping the emulation is backend code in gpui_linux's
private X11 client, which neither a Parallax crate nor an extension trait can reach. cfg: the X11 module is already
Linux/FreeBSD with the `x11` feature.

Upstream PR: not filed yet.

